41129727|t|RORgamma Hijacks HIF-1alpha to Promote Peritoneal Metastasis of Gastric Cancer.
41129727|a|Peritoneal metastasis (PM) is the most common and lethal form of metastasis in gastric cancer (GCa), highlighting an urgent need for treatment strategies. Here, we identified RORgamma as a key driver of PM in GCa tumors. Integrative bioinformatics and immunohistochemical analyses revealed aberrant upregulation of RORgamma in GCa tumors, particularly in metastatic lesions. Functionally, elevated RORgamma promoted GCa cell adaptation to hypoxia and accelerated PM formation in vivo. The hypoxic microenvironment in GCa stimulated RORgamma expression, which in turn enhanced HIF-1alpha stability, establishing a positive feedforward loop. Mechanistically, RORgamma interacted with HIF-1alpha and protected it from binding PHD3 and VHL, resulting in markedly decreased HIF-1alpha hydroxylation and ubiquitylation and increased HIF-1alpha accumulation, nuclear translocation, and transactivation. The RORgamma-HIF-1alpha complex bound and activated target genes involved in hypoxic response and epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), thereby driving GCa tumor growth and PM. Disruption of the RORgamma-HIF-1alpha interaction using RORgamma antagonists markedly promoted HIF-1alpha degradation and diminished its function. Importantly, in multiple GCa xenograft models, RORgamma inhibition effectively blocked tumor growth and PM while sensitizing tumors to chemotherapy. Thus, this study uncovers a mechanism of hypoxia-mediated metastasis through a RORgamma-HIF-1alpha reciprocal regulatory loop and offers a potential therapeutic option against PM in advanced cancer.
